51
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:02,719
from its sampled version.

52
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:05,439
Here's the key idea. We start with a

53
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:07,280
continuous signal f of t shown here.

54
00:02:05,439 --> 00:02:08,959
When we sample it at regular time

55
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:11,520
intervals, let's call that interval

56
00:02:08,959 --> 00:02:14,319
delta t, we obtain a discrete sequence

57
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:16,879
of values. The sampling theorem tells us

58
00:02:14,319 --> 00:02:19,040
that if certain conditions are met, we

59
00:02:16,879 --> 00:02:21,360
can reconstruct the original continuous

60
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:24,560
signal from these samples without losing

61
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:26,560
any information.

62
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:28,720
That's remarkable because even though we

63
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:31,040
only record values at specific time

64
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:33,440
points and there's missing information

65
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:36,080
in between, the theorem guarantees

66
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:38,160
perfect recovery. But this only works if

67
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:40,480
the original signal's 4A transform is

68
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:42,560
band limited. That means its frequency

69
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:47,440
spectrum is essentially zero outside a

70
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:49,440
certain range from minus W to plus W.

71
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:51,599
In the frequency domain, sampling

72
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:54,239
corresponds to a convolution between the

73
00:02:51,599 --> 00:02:56,959
original continuous forier spectrum and

74
00:02:54,239 --> 00:02:59,040
a periodic train of delta functions.

75
00:02:56,959 --> 00:03:00,959
This delta train in frequency comes from

76
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:03,360
the forier transform of the impulse

77
00:03:00,959 --> 00:03:06,000
train in time. The spacing between the

78
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:07,760
deltas in frequency, which we'll call P,

79
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,239
is the reciprocal of the sampling

80
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:12,400
interval delta T.

81
00:03:10,239 --> 00:03:15,280
To avoid any distortion, P must be at

82
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:17,200
least 2 W. This ensures there is no

83
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,519
overlap between the repeated copies of

84
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:21,280
the spectrum in the frequency domain.

85
00:03:19,519 --> 00:03:23,599
You can think of each delta function as

86
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:25,519
a copying machine. Each one shifts and

87
00:03:23,599 --> 00:03:28,080
reproduces the original spectrum at

88
00:03:25,519 --> 00:03:30,080
regular intervals. If the copies don't

89
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:32,560
overlap, the original information is

90
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:34,640
preserved perfectly.

91
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:36,879
In practice, we can apply a lowass

92
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:39,120
filter to keep only the central copy of

93
00:03:36,879 --> 00:03:41,519
the spectrum. Then perform an inverse

94
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:45,200
forier transform to reconstruct the

95
00:03:41,519 --> 00:03:47,120
exact original signal.

96
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:49,840
Now, a small note about the special case

97
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:52,159
where P equals exactly 2W. Here the

98
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:54,159
copies just touch at a single point.

99
00:03:52,159 --> 00:03:56,400
This doesn't cause problems unless that

100
00:03:54,159 --> 00:03:58,799
single point is a delta function with a

101
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:01,280
finite value, in which case it could

102
00:03:58,799 --> 00:04:03,280
contribute to the result. But in most

103
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:07,239
practical signals that single point has

104
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:07,239
no significant effect.

105
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:13,760
Now let's look at the special case where

106
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:15,840
P equals exactly 2 W. Suppose our

107
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:18,320
continuous signal is a pure sine wave

108
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:20,400
with a frequency of 1 herz. That means

109
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:22,720
it completes exactly one cycle in 1

110
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:25,120
second. In the frequency domain, this is

111
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:27,759
a band limited signal. Its maximum

112
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:29,759
frequency is 1 herz.

113
00:04:27,759 --> 00:04:32,080
If we sample at exactly twice that

114
00:04:29,759 --> 00:04:34,800
frequency, so at two hertz, we meet the

115
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:37,120
Nyquist rate exactly. In this case, we

116
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:39,280
take two samples within each cycle. For

117
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,960
a pure sinosoid, two unknowns fully

118
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:44,240
describe it, the amplitude and the

119
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:45,919
phase. In theory, two samples per cycle

120
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,919
should be enough to determine those two

121
00:04:45,919 --> 00:04:48,919
unknowns.

122
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:53,759
However, here's the problem.

123
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:56,000
Depending on where the samples fall, we

124
00:04:53,759 --> 00:04:58,560
might end up with the exact same sample

125
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,720
value each time. For example, always

126
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:03,520
sampling at the zero crossings of the

127
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:05,520
sine wave. In that case, all our samples

128
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:07,520
would be zero and we would have no

129
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:10,160
reliable way to determine the original

130
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:12,880
signal.

131
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:14,880
In the forier domain, a pure sine wave

132
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:16,960
corresponds to a pair of delta functions

133
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:19,600
located at its positive and negative

134
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:22,000
frequency components. When we sample at

135
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:23,680
exactly 2 W, the duplicated spectra in

136
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,560
the frequency domain just touch each

137
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:29,039
other. If the touching points happen to

138
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,639
be delta functions, those deltas overlap

139
00:05:29,039 --> 00:05:32,560
and we can no longer separate the

140
00:05:30,639 --> 00:05:34,320
contributions from each one.

141
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:37,360
Mathematically, it's like having the

142
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:40,240
equation x + y equals c. You know the

143
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:42,960
sum, but not the individual values. This

144
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:45,039
is an aliasing problem.

145
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:47,759
That's why in practice sampling exactly

146
00:05:45,039 --> 00:05:50,560
at 2 w is risky.

147
00:05:47,759 --> 00:05:52,720
To be safe, we require PPP, the spacing

148
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:55,600
between repeated spectra and frequency

149
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:57,440
to be slightly greater than 2W. In other

150
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:59,360
words, the sampling rate should be a

151
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:01,520
little higher than twice the maximum

152
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:03,440
frequency in the signal. This extra

153
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:07,880
margin ensures that no overlap occurs

154
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:07,880
and reconstruction remains reliable.

155
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:14,880
Here we have the whole derivation of the

156
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:17,440
sampling theorem summarized on one page.

157
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:19,120
You see a few steps underlined in red.

158
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:21,120
These are the important turning points

159
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:24,080
in the math, the places where something

160
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:25,919
key happens. In a detailed lecture, we

161
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:27,680
would walk through each of them slowly.

162
00:06:25,919 --> 00:06:30,400
But here, I want to focus on the big

163
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:32,560
picture.

164
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:34,479
We start in the frequency domain where

165
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:37,680
the spectrum of our signal is multiplied

166
00:06:34,479 --> 00:06:39,520
by a periodic train of impulses.

167
00:06:37,680 --> 00:06:41,759
When we bring this back to the time

168
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:43,840
domain, that multiplication turns into a

169
00:06:41,759 --> 00:06:46,080
