00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:50,600
results match what we would get in the

558
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:50,600
continuous world.

559
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:55,520
Let's now bring everything together in

560
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:57,200
one big picture, but with a bit more

561
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:59,600
detail.

562
00:21:57,200 --> 00:22:01,919
We start with a continuous function f of

563
00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:04,559
t and its continuous forier spectrum

564
00:22:01,919 --> 00:22:06,400
capital f of u. Our goal is to sample

565
00:22:04,559 --> 00:22:08,159
both the time domain and the frequency

566
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:10,559
domain in a way that makes sense for

567
00:22:08,159 --> 00:22:12,400
digital processing.

568
00:22:10,559 --> 00:22:15,919
First, let's ask how many samples will

569
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:18,559
we have in the time domain.

570
00:22:15,919 --> 00:22:21,039
This depends on two things. The sampling

571
00:22:18,559 --> 00:22:23,200
interval in time delta t. This is equal

572
00:22:21,039 --> 00:22:25,600
to 1 over capital p where capital p is

573
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:27,679
the spacing in the frequency domain. The

574
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:29,840
smaller the delta t, the more data

575
00:22:27,679 --> 00:22:32,000
points we collect.

576
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:34,320
The total duration of the signal capital

577
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:36,559
t, the longer we record, the more

578
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:38,640
samples we have.

579
00:22:36,559 --> 00:22:41,120
The total number of samples in the time

580
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:45,520
domain, call it n, is simply the total

581
00:22:41,120 --> 00:22:47,280
duration t / delta t. Since delta t is 1

582
00:22:45,520 --> 00:22:52,640
/ p,

583
00:22:47,280 --> 00:22:54,240
we can write n= t * p.

584
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:56,000
Now let's switch to the frequency

585
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:58,159
domain.

586
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:00,480
Here the total span of one period in

587
00:22:58,159 --> 00:23:02,559
frequency is capital p. The sampling

588
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:05,280
interval in frequency delta is equal to

589
00:23:02,559 --> 00:23:07,440
1 over capital t. So the total number of

590
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:10,320
samples in frequency over one period

591
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:14,480
call it M is the total span P divided by

592
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:16,880
deltaU. That's again P multiplied by T.

593
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:18,960
This symmetry is important. The number

594
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:20,480
of data points in the time domain and

595
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:22,880
the number of data points in the

596
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:26,640
frequency domain are the same both equal

597
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:28,720
to P multiplied by T.

598
00:23:26,640 --> 00:23:30,720
So whether we're sampling in time or in

599
00:23:28,720 --> 00:23:32,320
frequency, the total number of samples

600
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:34,080
is determined by the product of the

601
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:36,799
total span in one domain and the

602
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:38,640
sampling density in the other.

603
00:23:36,799 --> 00:23:41,039
This is the balanced relationship that

604
00:23:38,640 --> 00:23:43,039
underlies the discrete forier transform

605
00:23:41,039 --> 00:23:45,280
and it's why we can move between time

606
00:23:43,039 --> 00:23:46,960
and frequency representations so

607
00:23:45,280 --> 00:23:49,960
efficiently in digital signal

608
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:49,960
processing.

609
00:23:51,679 --> 00:23:55,200
Now let's highlight the key variables

610
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:58,240
and the important relationship between

611
00:23:55,200 --> 00:24:01,440
them. In the time domain, the total

612
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:03,760
number of samples is t * p.

613
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:05,840
Here capital t is the total duration of

614
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:08,240
the signal and capital p is the spacing

615
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:11,120
in the frequency domain. So we have n

616
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:12,880
equal to t multiplied by p.

617
00:24:11,120 --> 00:24:15,039
In the frequency domain, we have the

618
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:17,840
exact same number of samples also t

619
00:24:15,039 --> 00:24:20,080
multiplied by p. We call this number m.

620
00:24:17,840 --> 00:24:22,559
And here P is the total span of one

621
00:24:20,080 --> 00:24:25,360
period in frequency while T controls the

622
00:24:22,559 --> 00:24:27,440
sampling density in frequency.

623
00:24:25,360 --> 00:24:30,240
If we take the reciprocal of N, we get a

624
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:33,520
very useful relationship. 1 / N equals

625
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:35,840
delta T * deltaU where deltat t is the

626
00:24:33,520 --> 00:24:39,039
sampling step size in time and deltaU is

627
00:24:35,840 --> 00:24:40,799
the sampling step size and frequency.

628
00:24:39,039 --> 00:24:43,600
This is an expression of the duality

629
00:24:40,799 --> 00:24:46,159
between time and frequency.

630
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:48,320
If we fix the total number of samples, n

631
00:24:46,159 --> 00:24:50,559
making delta t smaller, meaning we

632
00:24:48,320 --> 00:24:52,880
sample more finely in time, will make

633
00:24:50,559 --> 00:24:55,440
deltaU larger, meaning the frequency

634
00:24:52,880 --> 00:24:58,559
samples are spaced farther apart. And

635
00:24:55,440 --> 00:25:00,559
the reverse is also true. In simple

636
00:24:58,559 --> 00:25:02,400
terms, if you zoom in more on one

637
00:25:00,559 --> 00:25:04,480
domain, you automatically zoom out in

638
00:25:02,400 --> 00:25:06,640
the other. This balance is built into

639
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:08,480
the mathematics of the forier transform

640
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:11,799
and is something we'll use later when we

641
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:11,799
look at applications.

642
00:25:13,679 --> 00:25:16,880
Now we're ready to move from the

643
00:25:15,039 --> 00:25:19,600
continuous Foriera transform to the

644
00:25:16,880 --> 00:25:21,760
discrete forier transform.

645
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:23,679
When we sample a continuous signal, it

646
00:25:21,760 --> 00:25:26,559
stops being a smooth curve that exists

647
00:25:23,679 --> 00:25:28,400
at every moment in time.

648
00:25:26,559 --> 00:25:30,480
Instead, it becomes the product of the

649
00:25:28,400 --> 00:25:32,400
original signal and a series of equally

650
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:35,039
spaced spikes at what we call delta

651
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:37,360
functions. These spikes occur at time

652
00:25:35,039 --> 00:25:39,520
intervals of deltat t, which means each

653
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:42,000
spike is separated by 1 divided by

654
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:43,600
capital p in time.

655
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:45,440
In other words, we only keep the

656
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:48,400
signal's value at these regular time

657
00:25:45,440 --> 00:25:50,480
points and everywhere else is zero.

658
00:25:48,400 --> 00:25:52,960
We can describe this mathematically as a

659
00:25:50,480 --> 00:25:56,000
sum of delta spikes.

660
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:58,640
Each spike occurs at a time equal to n /

661
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:00,960
p, where n goes from 0 up to n minus

662
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:03,120
one. The height of each spike is simply

663
00:26:00,960 --> 00:26:05,760
the value of the original signal at that

664
00:26:03,120 --> 00:26:07,600
time. You can imagine it as a row of

665
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:09,600
vertical lines each carrying the

666
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:12,240
amplitude of the signal at its sampling

667
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:14,080
point.

668
00:26:12,240 --> 00:26:16,320
This gives us the discrete version of

669
00:26:14,080 --> 00:26:17,840
the signal in the time domain. Even

670
00:26:16,320 --> 00:26:19,600
though it's just a set of separate

671
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:21,600
values, it still represents a function

672
00:26:19,600 --> 00:26:23,520
of time. So we can take its fur

673
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:25,520
transform.

674
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:27,600
When we do that, the furier transform

675
00:26:25,520 --> 00:26:30,320
turns into a sum over all the sampled

676
00:26:27,600 --> 00:26:32,880
points. The oscillating factor, what we

677
00:26:30,320 --> 00:26:35,600
call the kernel, still has the form e to

678
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:39,200
the power of minus j * 2<unk>i * the

679
00:26:35,600 --> 00:26:40,960
frequency u * n / p. But now we only

680
00:26:39,200 --> 00:26:42,559
evaluate it at those sampled time

681
00:26:40,960 --> 00:26:44,400
points.

682
00:26:42,559 --> 00:26:47,039
The outcome is what we call the direct

683
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:48,880
forier transform of the sampled signal.

684
00:26:47,039 --> 00:26:51,200
This is how we connect the continuous

685
00:26:48,880 --> 00:26:53,360
theory to the practical digital form of

686
00:26:51,200 --> 00:26:56,840
the discrete forier transform that we

687
00:26:53,360 --> 00:26:56,840
use in computation.

688
00:26:58,559 --> 00:27:02,159
The discrete forier transform we just

689
00:27:00,480 --> 00:27:05,520
discussed is actually a periodic

690
00:27:02,159 --> 00:27:08,720
function. Let's understand why.

691
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:11,039
Think back to the fier series.

692
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:13,279
In the time domain, a furier series is

693
00:27:11,039 --> 00:27:16,240
built from sinosoidal components. Each

694
00:27:13,279 --> 00:27:17,919
one a sine or cosine wave. And by

695
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:20,080
definition, each of these components is

696
00:27:17,919 --> 00:27:22,159
periodic. Whether we have just one

697
00:27:20,080 --> 00:27:25,279
frequency or many, the result will still

698
00:27:22,159 --> 00:27:27,200
be periodic.

699
00:27:25,279 --> 00:27:29,360
Now in this expression at the top the

700
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:31,600
left hand side shows the forier series

701
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:34,240
in the time domain with terms involving

702
00:27:31,600 --> 00:27:36,240
t over capital t.

703
00:27:34,240 --> 00:27:38,480
On the right hand side we have an almost

704
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:41,120
identical formula but here the variable

705
00:27:38,480 --> 00:27:43,600
t has been replaced by u and t has been

706
00:27:41,120 --> 00:27:45,840
replaced by p. This tells us that in the

707
00:27:43,600 --> 00:27:47,440
frequency domain we also get a periodic

708
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:49,520
function.

709
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:51,279
The periodicity comes directly from the

710
00:27:49,520 --> 00:27:52,960
fact that the forier transform of a

711
00:27:51,279 --> 00:27:55,520
sampled signal is made up of these

712
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:58,080
repeating sinosoidal components. Each

713
00:27:55,520 --> 00:27:59,840
one repeats over and over giving us a

714
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:03,120
continuous function in the frequency

715
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:04,880
domain that is also periodic.

716
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:07,120
This is exactly what we saw in the big

717
00:28:04,880 --> 00:28:09,279
picture earlier. When we sample in one

718
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:13,399
domain, the forier transform becomes

719
00:28:09,279 --> 00:28:13,399
periodic in the other domain.

720
00:28:14,880 --> 00:28:18,960
Up to now we've mostly talked about

721
00:28:16,559 --> 00:28:21,039
sampling in the time domain. But we can

722
00:28:18,960 --> 00:28:22,720
also sample directly in the farier

723
00:28:21,039 --> 00:28:24,799
domain.

724
00:28:22,720 --> 00:28:28,640
Let's start with the forier transform of

725
00:28:24,799 --> 00:28:30,799
our signal fhat of u. If we multiply

726
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:33,120
this spectrum by a train of delta

727
00:28:30,799 --> 00:28:35,360
functions in the frequency domain just

728
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:36,960
like we did earlier in the time domain,

729
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:39,360
we get a sampled version of the

730
00:28:36,960 --> 00:28:41,679
spectrum. That means we only keep its

731
00:28:39,360 --> 00:28:44,559
values at certain frequency points which

732
00:28:41,679 --> 00:28:47,919
we can label as U0 u1 all the way up to

733
00:28:44,559 --> 00:28:49,919
u n minus one.

734
00:28:47,919 --> 00:28:52,880
Here the spacing between these frequency

735
00:28:49,919 --> 00:28:55,120
samples is 1 / capital t where capital t

736
00:28:52,880 --> 00:28:57,679
is the period in the time domain. The

737
00:28:55,120 --> 00:29:00,000
index m runs from 0 to n minus one just

738
00:28:57,679 --> 00:29:01,760
like it did in the time domain case. And

739
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:03,840
the total number of samples in the

740
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:08,399
frequency domain is the same as in the

741
00:29:03,840 --> 00:29:10,480
time domain both equal to capital n.

742
00:29:08,399 --> 00:29:13,039
Mathematically, we can write the sampled

743
00:29:10,480 --> 00:29:16,399
spectrum at position u m as a sum over

744
00:29:13,039 --> 00:29:19,039
all the time domain samples f of n / p

745
00:29:16,399 --> 00:29:21,360
multiplied by a complex exponential. The

746
00:29:19,039 --> 00:29:25,440
exponent has a nice symmetry. It's e to

747
00:29:21,360 --> 00:29:27,039
the^ of minus j 2<unk>i n * m / capital

748
00:29:25,440 --> 00:29:29,279
n.

749
00:29:27,039 --> 00:29:31,600
This symmetry is important. It shows

750
00:29:29,279 --> 00:29:33,600
that sampling in the forier domain has

751
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:36,159
exactly the same mathematical structure

752
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:38,240
as sampling in the time domain. The

753
00:29:36,159 --> 00:29:41,679
formulas look almost identical just with

754
00:29:38,240 --> 00:29:43,760
the roles of time and frequency swapped.

755
00:29:41,679 --> 00:29:46,480
This parallel is one of the reasons the

756
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:48,240
discrete forier transform is so elegant.

757
00:29:46,480 --> 00:29:50,240
It's the same process whether you're

758
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:53,799
thinking in terms of time samples or

759
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:53,799
frequency samples.

760
00:29:55,600 --> 00:29:59,360
Now we can state the discrete forier

761
00:29:57,440 --> 00:30:02,559
transform.

762
00:29:59,360 --> 00:30:07,919
We've sampled the signal at times tn= to

763
00:30:02,559 --> 00:30:10,480
n / capital p for n= to 01 n minus one.

764
00:30:07,919 --> 00:30:14,559
We'll read the spectrum at frequencies

765
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:19,039
um m= to m / capital t for m= to 0 1

766
00:30:14,559 --> 00:30:22,720
nus1 with n= to t * p.

767
00:30:19,039 --> 00:30:26,080
The DFT value at um M is sum from n= 0

768
00:30:22,720 --> 00:30:30,000
to n minus1 f of n / p into e to the

769
00:30:26,080 --> 00:30:31,840
power of minus j 2 pi mn /n.

770
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:35,039
This is just an inner product with a

771
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:37,279
complex sinosoid of frequency index m.

772
00:30:35,039 --> 00:30:39,120
Each output is complex. Its magnitude

773
00:30:37,279 --> 00:30:41,520
tells us how much of that frequency is

774
00:30:39,120 --> 00:30:43,279
present and its angle the phase tells us

775
00:30:41,520 --> 00:30:46,720
