coefficients to compute, the total work

1101
00:44:03,760 --> 00:44:08,960
is proportional to n^ squ.

1102
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:10,720
This n squared growth in computations

1103
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:12,960
was a big deal in the early days of

1104
00:44:10,720 --> 00:44:15,200
signal processing when n could be very

1105
00:44:12,960 --> 00:44:17,920
large. That's why the development of the

1106
00:44:15,200 --> 00:44:19,599
fastfor transform or fft was such a

1107
00:44:17,920 --> 00:44:22,599
breakthrough. It reduced this cost

1108
00:44:19,599 --> 00:44:22,599
dramatically.

1109
00:44:24,319 --> 00:44:29,680
The fast forier transform or FFT is an

1110
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:32,720
efficient algorithm for computing the

1111
00:44:29,680 --> 00:44:35,040
discrete forier transform.

1112
00:44:32,720 --> 00:44:37,839
It was published by Kulie and Tuki in

1113
00:44:35,040 --> 00:44:40,640
1965 and it completely changed the way

1114
00:44:37,839 --> 00:44:42,640
we do signal processing.

1115
00:44:40,640 --> 00:44:45,280
To give you an idea of its impact in

1116
00:44:42,640 --> 00:44:47,839
1969 analyzing

1117
00:44:45,280 --> 00:44:50,800
148 data points from a seismic trace

1118
00:44:47,839 --> 00:44:53,520
using the standard DFT took more than 13

1119
00:44:50,800 --> 00:44:55,920
hours. using the FFT on the exact same

1120
00:44:53,520 --> 00:45:00,640
machine, the same analysis took just 2.4

1121
00:44:55,920 --> 00:45:02,480
seconds. That's an enormous improvement.

1122
00:45:00,640 --> 00:45:05,839
I remember when I was in primary school,

1123
00:45:02,480 --> 00:45:08,160
a 2000 point seismic data analysis could

1124
00:45:05,839 --> 00:45:10,240
take more than a full day to process.

1125
00:45:08,160 --> 00:45:13,200
But with FFT, the same task could be

1126
00:45:10,240 --> 00:45:15,119
finished in under 3 seconds.

1127
00:45:13,200 --> 00:45:17,200
This kind of speed up is critical in

1128
00:45:15,119 --> 00:45:19,280
many applications, especially those that

1129
00:45:17,200 --> 00:45:21,280
need real-time results. Whether it's

1130
00:45:19,280 --> 00:45:23,680
analyzing signals from an airplane

1131
00:45:21,280 --> 00:45:25,040
sensors, monitoring seismic activity, or

1132
00:45:23,680 --> 00:45:27,040
performing real-time image

1133
00:45:25,040 --> 00:45:29,440
reconstruction,

1134
00:45:27,040 --> 00:45:32,800
the reason FFT is so powerful is that it

1135
00:45:29,440 --> 00:45:35,599
reduces the computational complexity.

1136
00:45:32,800 --> 00:45:38,000
A naive DFT requires about n squared

1137
00:45:35,599 --> 00:45:41,119
operations. That's n multiplications and

1138
00:45:38,000 --> 00:45:44,400
additions for each of the n outputs.

1139
00:45:41,119 --> 00:45:49,440
The FFT reduces this to n* log base 2 of

1140
00:45:44,400 --> 00:45:52,079
n operations. That's a huge difference.

1141
00:45:49,440 --> 00:45:54,640
For example, if n is 1,000, the log base

1142
00:45:52,079 --> 00:45:56,720
2 of n is about 10. So instead of doing

1143
00:45:54,640 --> 00:45:59,280
a million operations, we only need about

1144
00:45:56,720 --> 00:46:01,920
10,000. The larger n gets, the more

1145
00:45:59,280 --> 00:46:04,480
dramatic the savings become.

1146
00:46:01,920 --> 00:46:06,720
Because of this efficiency, FFT still

1147
00:46:04,480 --> 00:46:09,040
plays an essential role in modern signal

1148
00:46:06,720 --> 00:46:10,960
processing, and it's also widely used in

1149
00:46:09,040 --> 00:46:12,560
areas like medical imaging, machine

1150
00:46:10,960 --> 00:46:15,560
learning, and convolutionbased

1151
00:46:12,560 --> 00:46:15,560
computations.

1152
00:46:17,440 --> 00:46:21,359
Because the inverse discrete forier

1153
00:46:19,440 --> 00:46:23,440
transform is so similar to the forward

1154
00:46:21,359 --> 00:46:25,920
transform, we can design a fast

1155
00:46:23,440 --> 00:46:29,280
algorithm for it as well. This is called

1156
00:46:25,920 --> 00:46:31,680
the inverse FFT or IFF.

1157
00:46:29,280 --> 00:46:34,480
In mat lab, the FFT function computes

1158
00:46:31,680 --> 00:46:37,359
the forward FFT and the iff function

1159
00:46:34,480 --> 00:46:41,280
computes the inverse FFT. For example,

1160
00:46:37,359 --> 00:46:43,520
FFT of XN computes an endpoint FFT. If

1161
00:46:41,280 --> 00:46:45,520
the vector X has fewer than N points, it

1162
00:46:43,520 --> 00:46:48,319
pads with zeros. If it has more, it

1163
00:46:45,520 --> 00:46:51,359
truncates. The IFFT works the same way,

1164
00:46:48,319 --> 00:46:53,200
just in reverse.

1165
00:46:51,359 --> 00:46:55,520
If we were to implement the forier

1166
00:46:53,200 --> 00:46:57,920
transform exactly as in the mathematical

1167
00:46:55,520 --> 00:47:01,040
definition, we would do a summation for

1168
00:46:57,920 --> 00:47:03,040
each frequency index k. Each summation

1169
00:47:01,040 --> 00:47:05,040
is essentially an inner product. It

1170
00:47:03,040 --> 00:47:07,280
requires n multiplications and n

1171
00:47:05,040 --> 00:47:10,240
additions. And we need to do this for

1172
00:47:07,280 --> 00:47:14,400
each of the n output points. That's n *

1173
00:47:10,240 --> 00:47:16,560
n operations, n^ squ in total.

1174
00:47:14,400 --> 00:47:18,480
The FFT and IFFF are like built-in

1175
00:47:16,560 --> 00:47:20,079
shortcuts.

1176
00:47:18,480 --> 00:47:22,319
You don't need to know all the details

1177
00:47:20,079 --> 00:47:24,079
of how they work to use them. Just like

1178
00:47:22,319 --> 00:47:25,760
you don't need to know how your phone

1179
00:47:24,079 --> 00:47:28,560
connects to your friend when you press

1180
00:47:25,760 --> 00:47:31,280
the call button, you can treat FFT and

1181
00:47:28,560 --> 00:47:33,040
if blackboxes, you give them the data

1182
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:34,800
and they give you the result quickly and

1183
00:47:33,040 --> 00:47:38,480
efficiently.

1184
00:47:34,800 --> 00:47:41,359
With FFT and IFFT, Fieralis becomes

1185
00:47:38,480 --> 00:47:43,440
practical for realtime work.

1186
00:47:41,359 --> 00:47:46,000
Once we can efficiently move between the

1187
00:47:43,440 --> 00:47:47,520
time domain and the frequency domain, we

1188
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:49,920
can use this power for many

1189
00:47:47,520 --> 00:47:52,000
applications. Performing convolution,

1190
00:47:49,920 --> 00:47:53,839
estimating spectra, removing noise,

1191
00:47:52,000 --> 00:47:57,640
detecting patterns or contours in

1192
00:47:53,839 --> 00:47:57,640
images, and much more.

1193
00:47:59,040 --> 00:48:02,640
Let's look at our first example. How to

1194
00:48:00,880 --> 00:48:05,760
compute convolution using the fast

1195
00:48:02,640 --> 00:48:07,599
forier transform or FFT.

1196
00:48:05,760 --> 00:48:10,000
You already know the direct method, the

1197
00:48:07,599 --> 00:48:12,000
one we practiced in class. You take one

1198
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:14,240
sequence, flip it, shift it, multiply

1199
00:48:12,000 --> 00:48:16,560
corresponding terms and sum them up.

1200
00:48:14,240 --> 00:48:18,480
Then you repeat for each shift. This

1201
00:48:16,560 --> 00:48:20,480
gives the convolution directly in the

1202
00:48:18,480 --> 00:48:22,400
time domain.

1203
00:48:20,480 --> 00:48:24,720
If the first sequence has length n and

1204
00:48:22,400 --> 00:48:27,760
the second has length m, the convolution

1205
00:48:24,720 --> 00:48:29,200
result has n + m minus one points.

1206
00:48:27,760 --> 00:48:31,520
That's how many shifts we need to

1207
00:48:29,200 --> 00:48:33,280
perform. And in terms of computation,

1208
00:48:31,520 --> 00:48:35,440
this direct method takes time

1209
00:48:33,280 --> 00:48:38,160
proportional to n squares, where n is

1210
00:48:35,440 --> 00:48:40,079
roughly the size of the data.

1211
00:48:38,160 --> 00:48:42,400
But there's a much faster indirect

1212
00:48:40,079 --> 00:48:44,160
method. Instead of doing the convolution

1213
00:48:42,400 --> 00:48:48,880
directly in the time domain, we switch

1214
00:48:44,160 --> 00:48:51,200
to the frequency domain using the FFT.

1215
00:48:48,880 --> 00:48:53,520
Here's the mat lab approach. First

1216
00:48:51,200 --> 00:48:55,839
compute the FFT of X. That gives us its

1217
00:48:53,520 --> 00:48:58,240
frequency spectrum.

1218
00:48:55,839 --> 00:49:01,440
Then compute the FFT of Y, the spectrum

1219
00:48:58,240 --> 00:49:03,280
of the filter or second sequence.

1220
00:49:01,440 --> 00:49:05,760
In the frequency domain, convolution

1221
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:07,760
turns into multiplication. So we simply

1222
00:49:05,760 --> 00:49:09,359
multiply the two spectra element by

1223
00:49:07,760 --> 00:49:11,760
element.

1224
00:49:09,359 --> 00:49:14,880
Finally apply the inverse FFT to that

1225
00:49:11,760 --> 00:49:16,640
product to return to the time domain.

1226
00:49:14,880 --> 00:49:18,640
This works because of the convolution

1227
00:49:16,640 --> 00:49:20,720
theorem. It says convolution in the time

1228
00:49:18,640 --> 00:49:23,440
domain is equivalent to multiplication

1229
00:49:20,720 --> 00:49:25,200
in the frequency domain.

1230
00:49:23,440 --> 00:49:27,520
If you compare the results from the

1231
00:49:25,200 --> 00:49:30,720
direct method and the FFTbased method in

1232
00:49:27,520 --> 00:49:33,440
mat lab, you'll see they match exactly.

1233
00:49:30,720 --> 00:49:36,720
The key difference is efficiency. Direct

1234
00:49:33,440 --> 00:49:39,359
convolution about n square operations.

1235
00:49:36,720 --> 00:49:42,880
FFTbased convolution about n log n

1236
00:49:39,359 --> 00:49:45,680
operations much faster for large n. One

1237
00:49:42,880 --> 00:49:47,599
small detail in discrete forier analysis

1238
00:49:45,680 --> 00:49:50,240
both the time domain and the frequency

1239
00:49:47,599 --> 00:49:52,960
domain are treated as periodic. That

1240
00:49:50,240 --> 00:49:55,040
means this fftbased convolution actually

1241
00:49:52,960 --> 00:49:58,839
performs circular convolution unless you

1242
00:49:55,040 --> 00:49:58,839
handle padding carefully.

1243
00:50:00,319 --> 00:50:04,880
Let's look at a second example. And

1244
00:50:02,079 --> 00:50:06,800
here's where things get interesting.

1245
00:50:04,880 --> 00:50:09,040
We again compute convolution in two

1246
00:50:06,800 --> 00:50:11,119
ways. First, the direct method in the

1247
00:50:09,040 --> 00:50:13,359
time domain.

1248
00:50:11,119 --> 00:50:15,280
Second, the FFTbased method multiplying

1249
00:50:13,359 --> 00:50:18,319
in the frequency domain and taking the

1250
00:50:15,280 --> 00:50:21,920
inverse FFT. This time the results are

1251
00:50:18,319 --> 00:50:23,520
different. Why?

1252
00:50:21,920 --> 00:50:26,240
It comes down to the fact that in

1253
00:50:23,520 --> 00:50:28,559
discrete furier analysis, our signals

1254
00:50:26,240 --> 00:50:31,200
are treated as periodic, not single

1255
00:50:28,559 --> 00:50:33,760
isolated sequences, but infinitely

1256
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:36,000
repeated copies.

1257
00:50:33,760 --> 00:50:37,839
Imagine X as one segment in time and Y

1258
00:50:36,000 --> 00:50:39,760
as another.

1259
00:50:37,839 --> 00:50:41,520
In the continuous world, when we shift

1260
00:50:39,760 --> 00:50:43,359
one relative to the other, we only

1261
00:50:41,520 --> 00:50:45,680
overlap the part that exists in the

1262
00:50:43,359 --> 00:50:47,839
original copy. But in the discrete

1263
00:50:45,680 --> 00:50:49,760
furier model, when you shift, parts of

1264
00:50:47,839 --> 00:50:52,480
one copy can wrap around and overlap

1265
00:50:49,760 --> 00:50:54,559
with the start of the other copy.

1266
00:50:52,480 --> 00:50:56,960
It's as if your neighbor signal is

1267
00:50:54,559 --> 00:50:58,559
spilling into your yard and at the same

1268
00:50:56,960 --> 00:51:01,359
time you're spilling into your other

1269
00:50:58,559 --> 00:51:03,440
neighbor's yard on the left.

1270
00:51:01,359 --> 00:51:06,079
That wraparound interaction is exactly

1271
00:51:03,440 --> 00:51:07,760
what circular convolution means. The

1272
00:51:06,079 --> 00:51:09,920
signals are treated as if arranged

1273
00:51:07,760 --> 00:51:11,760
around a circle.

1274
00:51:09,920 --> 00:51:13,920
When you slide one past the end, it

1275
00:51:11,760 --> 00:51:16,240
reappears at the other side. The

1276
00:51:13,920 --> 00:51:18,559
FFTbased convolution performs this

1277
00:51:16,240 --> 00:51:20,240
circular convolution by default which is

1278
00:51:18,559 --> 00:51:23,200
why the result here doesn't match the

1279
00:51:20,240 --> 00:51:24,960
direct linear convolution.

1280
00:51:23,200 --> 00:51:27,280
Later we'll see how to avoid this

1281
00:51:24,960 --> 00:51:31,720
wraparound effect. So the FFT method

1282
00:51:27,280 --> 00:51:31,720
matches the true linear convolution.

1283
00:51:33,440 --> 00:51:38,319
We just saw that FFTbased convolution

1284
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:40,319
gives us circular convolution by default

1285
00:51:38,319 --> 00:51:42,480
where the neighboring copies wrap around

1286
00:51:40,319 --> 00:51:43,920
and interfere.

1287
00:51:42,480 --> 00:51:45,599
The simplest way to avoid that

1288
00:51:43,920 --> 00:51:47,440
wraparound is to make sure those

1289
00:51:45,599 --> 00:51:49,760
neighbors are far enough away so they

1290
00:51:47,440 --> 00:51:52,240
can't overlap during the shift. That's

1291
00:51:49,760 --> 00:51:54,319
what zero padding does.

1292
00:51:52,240 --> 00:51:56,400
We take our original signals and append

1293
00:51:54,319 --> 00:51:59,760
enough zeros to both so that the total

1294
00:51:56,400 --> 00:52:01,760
length is at least n + m minus one where

1295
00:51:59,760 --> 00:52:04,000
n and m are the original lengths of the

1296
00:52:01,760 --> 00:52:05,920
two sequences.

1297
00:52:04,000 --> 00:52:07,920
In this example, each signal is only

1298
00:52:05,920 --> 00:52:11,280
five samples long, but we pad them out

1299
00:52:07,920 --> 00:52:13,839
to length 16.

1300
00:52:11,280 --> 00:52:15,440
Now when we perform the FFT multiply in

1301
00:52:13,839 --> 00:52:18,000
the frequency domain and take the

1302
00:52:15,440 --> 00:52:20,400
inverse FFT the wraparound region is all

1303
00:52:18,000 --> 00:52:23,359
zeros. So the result matches the direct

1304
00:52:20,400 --> 00:52:25,440
linear convolution exactly.

1305
00:52:23,359 --> 00:52:27,280
In other words, zero padding is like

1306
00:52:25,440 --> 00:52:29,280
making your yard so wide that your

1307
00:52:27,280 --> 00:52:32,280
neighbors are too far away to cause any

1308
00:52:29,280 --> 00:52:32,280
trouble.

1309
00:52:33,760 --> 00:52:38,000
This picture shows the idea of zero

1310
00:52:35,359 --> 00:52:40,319
padding in a visual way.

1311
00:52:38,000 --> 00:52:42,319
on top. The red triangles represent one

1312
00:52:40,319 --> 00:52:44,079
signal and the blue triangles represent

1313
00:52:42,319 --> 00:52:45,680
the other.

1314
00:52:44,079 --> 00:52:48,000
Without padding, these shapes are

1315
00:52:45,680 --> 00:52:50,079
repeated periodically. So when you slide

1316
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:52,400
them for convolution, parts from the

1317
00:52:50,079 --> 00:52:54,000
neighboring copies wrap around and

1318
00:52:52,400 --> 00:52:58,079
interfere. That's the circular

1319
00:52:54,000 --> 00:53:00,000
convolution effect we saw earlier.

1320
00:52:58,079 --> 00:53:02,319
Now by adding enough zeros, we

1321
00:53:00,000 --> 00:53:04,000
artificially extend the period. This

1322
00:53:02,319 --> 00:53:05,520
pushes the neighboring copies farther

1323
00:53:04,000 --> 00:53:08,079
apart. So when we perform the

1324
00:53:05,520 --> 00:53:10,480
convolution only the single red copy and

1325
00:53:08,079 --> 00:53:12,000
the single blue copy overlap. The

1326
00:53:10,480 --> 00:53:13,839
neighbors never get close enough to

1327
00:53:12,000 --> 00:53:15,760
contribute.

1328
00:53:13,839 --> 00:53:17,520
The result is that our circular

1329
00:53:15,760 --> 00:53:19,760
convolution now matches the linear

1330
00:53:17,520 --> 00:53:22,319
convolution exactly because there's no

1331
00:53:19,760 --> 00:53:23,839
wraparound. The earlier mismatch happens

1332
00:53:22,319 --> 00:53:25,680
simply because we didn't have enough

1333
00:53:23,839 --> 00:53:27,520
zeros. So the neighbors still

1334
00:53:25,680 --> 00:53:29,839
overlapped.

1335
00:53:27,520 --> 00:53:31,839
This is the essence of zero padding.

1336
00:53:29,839 --> 00:53:33,359
Make the yard wide enough so your

1337
00:53:31,839 --> 00:53:36,359
neighbors can't mess up your

1338
00:53:33,359 --> 00:53:36,359
calculation.

1339
00:53:38,240 --> 00:53:42,160
If you'd like to explore these ideas

1340
00:53:40,160 --> 00:53:43,920
further, Math Works has some excellent

1341
00:53:42,160 --> 00:53:46,079
tutorials and examples in its

1342
00:53:43,920 --> 00:53:48,000
documentation.

1343
00:53:46,079 --> 00:53:50,400
This particular page walks through the

1344
00:53:48,000 --> 00:53:53,200
relationship between linear and circular

1345
00:53:50,400 --> 00:53:55,200
convolution in mat lab. It explains the

1346
00:53:53,200 --> 00:53:57,440
key conditions under which the two are

1347
00:53:55,200 --> 00:53:59,839
equivalent and shows how zero padding

1348
00:53:57,440 --> 00:54:03,119
can be used to make circular convolution

1349
00:53:59,839 --> 00:54:05,040
behave like linear convolution.

1350
00:54:03,119 --> 00:54:07,040
You'll also find clear mat lab examples

1351
00:54:05,040 --> 00:54:09,119
here. Creating vectors, padding them to

1352
00:54:07,040 --> 00:54:11,119
the right length, applying the FFT,

1353
00:54:09,119 --> 00:54:13,280
multiplying in the frequency domain, and

1354
