delta t fixed and the total number of

1369
00:54:53,920 --> 00:54:59,280
samples n fixed, then our frequency

1370
00:54:55,920 --> 00:55:01,200
spacing delta new is also fixed.

1371
00:54:59,280 --> 00:55:02,640
That means if a signal contains two

1372
00:55:01,200 --> 00:55:04,559
frequencies that are very close

1373
00:55:02,640 --> 00:55:07,760
together, the spectrum may not have

1374
00:55:04,559 --> 00:55:09,440
enough resolution to separate them.

1375
00:55:07,760 --> 00:55:11,839
One way to improve the resolution is

1376
00:55:09,440 --> 00:55:13,760
called zero padding.

1377
00:55:11,839 --> 00:55:15,920
This means we add zeros to the end of

1378
00:55:13,760 --> 00:55:18,240
the time domain signal which increases

1379
00:55:15,920 --> 00:55:20,240
the total number of samples n.

1380
00:55:18,240 --> 00:55:22,800
Increasing n makes the spacing in the

1381
00:55:20,240 --> 00:55:24,960
frequency domain delta new smaller

1382
00:55:22,800 --> 00:55:27,680
giving us a finer frequency grid and

1383
00:55:24,960 --> 00:55:29,599
better spectral resolution.

1384
00:55:27,680 --> 00:55:32,079
In this mat lab example, we create a

1385
00:55:29,599 --> 00:55:34,720
signal by adding two sinosoids. The

1386
00:55:32,079 --> 00:55:38,480
first term is the cosine of 2 *<unk> *

1387
00:55:34,720 --> 00:55:42,240
100 * t. The second term is the s of 2 *

1388
00:55:38,480 --> 00:55:44,960
pi * 2002 2.5 * t.

1389
00:55:42,240 --> 00:55:46,640
We sample t from 0 to 1 second in steps

1390
00:55:44,960 --> 00:55:49,040
of 0.001

1391
00:55:46,640 --> 00:55:51,920
seconds, which corresponds to a sampling

1392
00:55:49,040 --> 00:55:54,160
frequency of 1 kohz. Without zero

1393
00:55:51,920 --> 00:55:56,319
padding, the frequency step size may be

1394
00:55:54,160 --> 00:56:00,559
too large to clearly separate the peaks

1395
00:55:56,319 --> 00:56:04,400
at 100 htz and 102.5 hertz. With zero

1396
00:56:00,559 --> 00:56:05,839
padding, the peaks become distinct.

1397
00:56:04,400 --> 00:56:07,520
If you want the full mat lab

1398
00:56:05,839 --> 00:56:11,240
walkthrough, you can follow the link at

1399
00:56:07,520 --> 00:56:11,240
the bottom of the slide.

1400
00:56:12,720 --> 00:56:15,920
Now let's see what happens when we

1401
00:56:14,160 --> 00:56:18,319
perform the forier transform without

1402
00:56:15,920 --> 00:56:20,720
using zero padding.

1403
00:56:18,319 --> 00:56:23,359
We take our signal X and compute its

1404
00:56:20,720 --> 00:56:25,040
discrete forier transform using the FFT

1405
00:56:23,359 --> 00:56:26,880
function.

1406
00:56:25,040 --> 00:56:28,559
We keep only the first half of the

1407
00:56:26,880 --> 00:56:30,160
spectrum because for a real valued

1408
00:56:28,559 --> 00:56:32,240
signal, the second half is just the

1409
00:56:30,160 --> 00:56:34,240
mirror image.

1410
00:56:32,240 --> 00:56:36,400
Then we normalize by dividing by the

1411
00:56:34,240 --> 00:56:39,040
length of X and scale the amplitudes

1412
00:56:36,400 --> 00:56:41,359
appropriately. The frequency axis is

1413
00:56:39,040 --> 00:56:43,040
calculated from 0 to half the sampling

1414
00:56:41,359 --> 00:56:44,960
rate in steps of the frequency

1415
00:56:43,040 --> 00:56:47,280
resolution.

1416
00:56:44,960 --> 00:56:49,520
On the plot, you see two peaks in blue.

1417
00:56:47,280 --> 00:56:51,200
The first peak is at exactly 100 hertz.

1418
00:56:49,520 --> 00:56:53,920
That one is well captured because it

1419
00:56:51,200 --> 00:56:56,160
matches the frequency grid.

1420
00:56:53,920 --> 00:56:59,200
The second component, however, is at

1421
00:56:56,160 --> 00:57:01,920
102.5 hertz, which does not align neatly

1422
00:56:59,200 --> 00:57:04,079
with the frequency samples.

1423
00:57:01,920 --> 00:57:06,079
As a result, the peak is lower than it

1424
00:57:04,079 --> 00:57:08,720
should be. You can see that it only

1425
00:57:06,079 --> 00:57:11,040
reaches about half of its true amplitude

1426
00:57:08,720 --> 00:57:12,880
and it spreads into nearby frequency

1427
00:57:11,040 --> 00:57:14,799
bins.

1428
00:57:12,880 --> 00:57:16,960
This illustrates the limitation of the

1429
00:57:14,799 --> 00:57:20,359
frequency resolution when we don't use

1430
00:57:16,960 --> 00:57:20,359
zero padding.

1431
00:57:21,839 --> 00:57:25,359
Now let's see what happens when we use

1432
00:57:23,359 --> 00:57:27,520
zero padding.

1433
00:57:25,359 --> 00:57:30,000
We take our original signal and extend

1434
00:57:27,520 --> 00:57:32,799
it in the time domain by adding a large

1435
00:57:30,000 --> 00:57:34,400
number of zeros at the end.

1436
00:57:32,799 --> 00:57:36,160
This does not change the actual

1437
00:57:34,400 --> 00:57:39,200
information in the signal, but it does

1438
00:57:36,160 --> 00:57:41,440
increase the total number of samples.

1439
00:57:39,200 --> 00:57:43,440
When we compute the forier transform of

1440
00:57:41,440 --> 00:57:45,119
this longer sequence, the frequency

1441
00:57:43,440 --> 00:57:47,760
spacing between the points in the

1442
00:57:45,119 --> 00:57:49,520
spectrum becomes smaller.

1443
00:57:47,760 --> 00:57:51,440
This means we get a finer frequency

1444
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:53,920
grid, more points between zero and the

1445
00:57:51,440 --> 00:57:56,000
Nyquist frequency.

1446
00:57:53,920 --> 00:57:58,319
As a result, when we plot the spectrum,

1447
00:57:56,000 --> 00:58:01,520
both peaks, the one at 100 htz and the

1448
00:57:58,319 --> 00:58:03,839
one at 102.5 htz are now represented

1449
00:58:01,520 --> 00:58:05,520
much more accurately.

1450
00:58:03,839 --> 00:58:07,280
You can see that their amplitudes are

1451
00:58:05,520 --> 00:58:09,040
correctly estimated and the peaks are

1452
00:58:07,280 --> 00:58:11,280
sharper.

1453
00:58:09,040 --> 00:58:13,280
So, zero padding is a simple but

1454
00:58:11,280 --> 00:58:15,680
powerful trick for improving the visual

1455
00:58:13,280 --> 00:58:17,200
resolution of your spectrum. It doesn't

1456
00:58:15,680 --> 00:58:18,960
actually add new information, but it

1457
00:58:17,200 --> 00:58:22,200
lets you see the frequency components

1458
00:58:18,960 --> 00:58:22,200
more clearly.

1459
00:58:23,760 --> 00:58:27,839
Before we wrap up, I want to highlight

1460
00:58:25,599 --> 00:58:30,160
something that often feels like magic in

1461
00:58:27,839 --> 00:58:32,480
signal processing.

1462
00:58:30,160 --> 00:58:35,359
A signal cannot be both time limited and

1463
00:58:32,480 --> 00:58:37,680
band limited at the same time.

1464
00:58:35,359 --> 00:58:39,599
If a signal is strictly limited in time,

1465
00:58:37,680 --> 00:58:41,920
meaning it exists only within a certain

1466
00:58:39,599 --> 00:58:44,559
interval, its frequency spectrum will

1467
00:58:41,920 --> 00:58:46,559
stretch infinitely. And if a signal is

1468
00:58:44,559 --> 00:58:48,559
strictly limited in frequency, meaning

1469
00:58:46,559 --> 00:58:50,799
it contains only a finite range of

1470
00:58:48,559 --> 00:58:53,520
frequencies, then it must extend

1471
00:58:50,799 --> 00:58:55,599
infinitely in time.

1472
00:58:53,520 --> 00:58:57,920
In practice, we often work with signals

1473
00:58:55,599 --> 00:59:00,960
that are approximately timelmited and

1474
00:58:57,920 --> 00:59:02,799
approximately band limited.

1475
00:59:00,960 --> 00:59:04,799
This approximation along with clever

1476
00:59:02,799 --> 00:59:06,880
techniques like sampling, zero padding,

1477
00:59:04,799 --> 00:59:09,680
and windowing allows us to do what might

1478
00:59:06,880 --> 00:59:11,359
seem impossible, turning the impossible

1479
00:59:09,680 --> 00:59:13,440
into something that works perfectly well

1480
00:59:11,359 --> 00:59:15,359
for our needs.

1481
00:59:13,440 --> 00:59:19,240
That's the kind of magic you see every

1482
00:59:15,359 --> 00:59:19,240
day in signal processing.

1483
00:59:20,880 --> 00:59:24,160
Now, let's step back and look at the big

1484
00:59:22,480 --> 00:59:26,079
picture.

1485
00:59:24,160 --> 00:59:27,760
Everything we've discussed so far seems

1486
00:59:26,079 --> 00:59:30,880
to work perfectly, but there's an

1487
00:59:27,760 --> 00:59:34,319
important approximation hidden here. We

1488
00:59:30,880 --> 00:59:36,559
often say if a signal is finite in time,

1489
00:59:34,319 --> 00:59:39,440
meaning it has a limited duration, then

1490
00:59:36,559 --> 00:59:41,839
its furier spectrum is also limited to a

1491
00:59:39,440 --> 00:59:45,119
finite interval.

1492
00:59:41,839 --> 00:59:47,440
In reality, that's not strictly true.

1493
00:59:45,119 --> 00:59:49,680
If a signal is perfectly finite in the

1494
00:59:47,440 --> 00:59:52,160
time domain, its spectrum will actually

1495
00:59:49,680 --> 00:59:53,760
extend infinitely. It will get smaller

1496
00:59:52,160 --> 00:59:57,839
and smaller as you move away from the

1497
00:59:53,760 --> 00:59:59,359
center, but it never truly stops.

1498
00:59:57,839 --> 01:00:02,640
You might argue that if we take the

1499
00:59:59,359 --> 01:00:04,640
frequency range from minus w to plus w

1500
01:00:02,640 --> 01:00:06,799
and make w large enough, the parts

1501
01:00:04,640 --> 01:00:09,040
outside this range become so small that

1502
01:00:06,799 --> 01:00:10,880
they can be ignored.

1503
01:00:09,040 --> 01:00:13,520
And yes, in practical engineering we

1504
01:00:10,880 --> 01:00:15,760
often do that. But from a mathematical

1505
01:00:13,520 --> 01:00:18,400
point of view, we have to be careful.

1506
01:00:15,760 --> 01:00:20,480
Why?

1507
01:00:18,400 --> 01:00:22,480
Because when we discretize the spectrum

1508
01:00:20,480 --> 01:00:26,079
is periodically repeated, we get

1509
01:00:22,480 --> 01:00:28,480
infinitely many copies of it. Even if a

1510
01:00:26,079 --> 01:00:30,799
single copy has very small values far

1511
01:00:28,480 --> 01:00:34,319
away from the center, those small values

1512
01:00:30,799 --> 01:00:36,880
from every copy can add up. The further

1513
01:00:34,319 --> 01:00:38,799
away a copy is, the less it contributes.

1514
01:00:36,880 --> 01:00:40,799
But since there are infinitely many of

1515
01:00:38,799 --> 01:00:42,319
them, the sum of all those tiny

1516
01:00:40,799 --> 01:00:44,160
contributions might still be

1517
01:00:42,319 --> 01:00:46,000
significant.

1518
01:00:44,160 --> 01:00:48,079
This is why convergence is not always

1519
01:00:46,000 --> 01:00:49,359
guaranteed and why mathematical rigor is

1520
01:00:48,079 --> 01:00:52,359
important when making these

1521
01:00:49,359 --> 01:00:52,359
approximations.

1522
01:00:54,319 --> 01:00:59,839
Let me give you a simple example to show

1523
01:00:56,160 --> 01:01:01,760
why getting smaller isn't always enough.

1524
01:00:59,839 --> 01:01:04,559
Imagine a curve where the height at any

1525
01:01:01,760 --> 01:01:07,200
point is equal to 1 / the position along

1526
01:01:04,559 --> 01:01:08,960
the horizontal axis.

1527
01:01:07,200 --> 01:01:11,040
As you move farther to the right, the

1528
01:01:08,960 --> 01:01:12,960
values get smaller and smaller. For

1529
01:01:11,040 --> 01:01:15,119
example, at position one, the value is

1530
01:01:12,960 --> 01:01:17,200
one. At position two, it's 1/2. At

1531
01:01:15,119 --> 01:01:20,960
position 3, it's 1/3. At position 4,

1532
01:01:17,200 --> 01:01:23,359
it's 1/4. And so on.

1533
01:01:20,960 --> 01:01:25,200
You might think these numbers are tiny.

1534
01:01:23,359 --> 01:01:27,119
If we keep adding them, the total should

1535
01:01:25,200 --> 01:01:29,119
stay small.

1536
01:01:27,119 --> 01:01:31,440
But here's the surprising fact. Even if

1537
01:01:29,119 --> 01:01:33,440
you start far out like at 1 over 1

1538
01:01:31,440 --> 01:01:35,920
million and then keep adding 1 over 2

1539
01:01:33,440 --> 01:01:39,040
million, 1 over 3 million and so on, the

1540
01:01:35,920 --> 01:01:40,720
total will still grow without limit.

1541
01:01:39,040 --> 01:01:42,880
In other words, an infinite number of

1542
01:01:40,720 --> 01:01:45,440
tiny contributions can still add up to

1543
01:01:42,880 --> 01:01:47,680
something infinitely large. And this is

1544
01:01:45,440 --> 01:01:50,319
exactly the kind of situation we face in

1545
01:01:47,680 --> 01:01:52,640
fur analysis. Very small contributions

1546
01:01:50,319 --> 01:01:54,400
from infinitely many frequency copies

1547
01:01:52,640 --> 01:01:57,400
can still combine into something

1548
01:01:54,400 --> 01:01:57,400
significant.

1549
01:01:58,960 --> 01:02:03,040
This idea connects to the famous wheat

1550
01:02:01,119 --> 01:02:06,720
and chessboard problem which is a

1551
01:02:03,040 --> 01:02:09,520
classic example of exponential growth.

1552
01:02:06,720 --> 01:02:11,359
Imagine a chessboard with 64 squares. On

1553
01:02:09,520 --> 01:02:13,680
the first square you place one grain of

1554
01:02:11,359 --> 01:02:15,680
wheat. On the next square you double it

1555
01:02:13,680 --> 01:02:17,520
two grains.

1556
01:02:15,680 --> 01:02:21,599
On the third square you double it again

1557
01:02:17,520 --> 01:02:23,359
four grains. Then 8 1632 and so on. By

1558
01:02:21,599 --> 01:02:24,799
the time you fill all the squares, the

1559
01:02:23,359 --> 01:02:27,520
total number of grains becomes

1560
01:02:24,799 --> 01:02:30,480
unimaginably large, enough to cover the

1561
01:02:27,520 --> 01:02:32,400
earth with a thick layer of wheat.

1562
01:02:30,480 --> 01:02:36,160
This illustrates how quickly exponential

1563
01:02:32,400 --> 01:02:38,240
growth can get out of control.

1564
01:02:36,160 --> 01:02:40,319
Now, in our forier problem, for our

1565
01:02:38,240 --> 01:02:43,119
approximation trick to work, the

1566
01:02:40,319 --> 01:02:45,599
leftover terms, those tiny contributions

1567
01:02:43,119 --> 01:02:47,760
from far away frequencies must shrink

1568
01:02:45,599 --> 01:02:49,839
faster than one divided by the position

1569
01:02:47,760 --> 01:02:51,520
number. That means the function must be

1570
01:02:49,839 --> 01:02:53,839
smooth enough so that its spectrum

1571
01:02:51,520 --> 01:02:55,920
decays quickly.

1572
01:02:53,839 --> 01:02:58,319
In fact, for most practical functions,

1573
01:02:55,920 --> 01:03:00,960
after some pre-processing and smoothing,

1574
01:02:58,319 --> 01:03:03,520
the decay is even faster, often like one

1575
01:03:00,960 --> 01:03:05,200
divided by the position number squared.

1576
01:03:03,520 --> 01:03:07,040
And in that case, adding up all the

1577
01:03:05,200 --> 01:03:09,280
small contributions still gives you a

1578
01:03:07,040 --> 01:03:12,760
small total. This is what allows our

1579
01:03:09,280 --> 01:03:12,760
approach to work.

1580
01:03:14,000 --> 01:03:17,760
So at this point we have completed the

1581
01:03:15,839 --> 01:03:19,680
main part of our discussion on forier

1582
01:03:17,760 --> 01:03:21,680
analysis.

